    "body": "### Problem\n\nAs in the title. This is more of a curiosity than a bug since why would anyone intentionally do this. It's not a buffer overflow either (so not a vulnerability), just an `abort()`.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\necho '# vim: iskeyword=1-100000000000000000000000:' > test.txt\nnvim test.txt\n```\n\nStacktrace roughly like this.\n\n```\n#3  0x00007iiiiiiiiiii in __GI_raise (sig=sig@entry=6) at ../sysdeps/posix/raise.c:26\n#4  0x00007iiiiiiiiiii in __GI_abort () at ./stdlib/abort.c:79\n#5  0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in parse_isopt.constprop ()\n#6  0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in buf_init_chartab.constprop ()\n#7  0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in did_set_iskeyword ()\n#8  0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in set_option.lto_priv ()\n#9  0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in do_set ()\n#10 0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in chk_modeline ()\n#11 0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in do_modelines ()\n#12 0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in open_buffer ()\n#13 0x00005iiiiiiiiiii in main ()\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nAn error is raised, for example.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.12.5\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\ntmux\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nscreen\n\n### Installation\n\ndownload from GitHub release",
